  "note": "Per-method MT coverage (supported languages) imported cite-only from each provider's published list; ISO 639-3. COUNT SEMANTICS (settled 2026-08-16 — read this before quoting any number): `count` is NOT one kind of quantity, so every entry now declares `countBasis`. 'publisher-list-rows' = the length of the provider's own published list exactly as they publish it (table rows / tags), cite-only — this is the figure public prose may attribute to the provider by name. 'champollion-derived-enumeration' = OUR collapse of their list to distinct ISO 639-3 base languages — never attributable to the provider (see CLAUDE.md derived-provenance rule). 'publisher-stated-headline' = a total the publisher asserts with no enumerable list behind it. `iso6393Count` always equals len(iso6393) and is always Champollion-derived; for 'publisher-list-rows' entries it is LOWER than `count` because script/region variants collapse (Google zh-CN+zh-TW→zho, NLLB's 200 FLORES variants→196 base codes, LibreTranslate zt/pb→zho/por). That gap is arithmetic, not a defect — it was misread as one in docs/PROVIDER_COUNT_VERIFICATION_2026-08-01.md §3.1. Map coverage and tier math read the iso6393 ARRAY, never `count`. tier: 'service' = deployable consumer/API service; 'open' = open research models. anyToAny: true = the method can intertranslate within its whole set; false (OPUS-MT, Tilde) = per-pair/per-engine models only — coverage of a language does NOT imply any given pair exists. NOT reproduced by Champollion. OPUS-MT iso6393 list pending (count cited). v2 (2026-07-22): releaseDate + releaseDate_source_url added where cleanly citable (Tilde/OPUS omitted — no verifiable date); omt1600 added COUNT-ONLY (cited, never joins map coverage/tier counts) with Meta’s own >400-of-1,600 quality self-report for the omnimodel-escalation timeline. INGEST NOTE 2026-08-10: entries carrying `supersededBy` are no longer ingested into the atlas — a fetched source now carries the same coverage from the owner’s own publication — but they REMAIN here because pre-cutover runtime consumers (tier logic, the queue ranker, the website) still read this file. They are deleted with the file itself at cutover, not before: retiring data ahead of its consumers is how a working CLI breaks in the name of tidiness.",
